  “Of course,” said Vale, pleasantly, with a pause I counted at seven
and a half. And, into the microphone, in the same unhurried voice,
“Let us continue. One.”
  The room said, “Two.”



I’ve thought a lot about what happened next.
    There was no plan. The idea arrived in my mouth at the exact mo‐
ment I opened it. I bent down to Manny, who was sitting stiff and
glassy-eyed behind his drum, in a tuxedo with a split seam, and I said
into his ear, very quietly:
    “Seven.”
    He blinked. His eyes found mine.
    “Seven,” I said. “Play seven. Under the count.”
    He didn’t ask. Manny has never once asked.
    He put his hand flat on the conga head and began, so softly that
only the people beside him could hear it, a pattern of seven.
    One-two-three, one-two, one-two. A little lopsided pulse, off the grid,
no more than a pair of fingers drumming on a table. It ran under the
count of eight like a mouse under a floor. And I watched the count, el‐
even hundred voices, lean toward it.
    “Five. Six. Seven—”
    “Eight,” said Papo, in the back, in a rusty voice.
    “Siete,” said Wilson.
    “Eight.”
    “It’s seven! I just heard it, he said seven, it’s seven!”
    “You can’t count! You never could!”
    “I can count better than your mother can cook!”
    A ripple went through the room. A laugh, somebody’s, started in
the middle and spread, rough, ugly, wonderful. The eleven hundred
